# Commits to skip when running `git blame`, so a mechanical reformat does
# not take credit for 136 files it only re-indented.
#
# Configure once per clone (this cannot be committed):
#
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# The hash below is only valid if this pull request lands with the
# reformat commit's hash intact. THREE operations destroy it, all
# silently:
#
#   * squash merge   -- collapses the branch into one new commit
#   * rebase merge   -- replays every commit with a new hash
#   * updating this branch with `git pull --rebase`, or GitHub's
#     "Update branch" when it offers rebase rather than merge
#
# This repository squash-merges by default, and the branch needed a
# conflict resolution, so the rebase paths are live risks and not
# theoretical. Update and merge with a MERGE COMMIT only.
#
# Git ignores an unknown hash here SILENTLY -- no error, no warning --
# and every reformatted file stays attributed to the reformat anyway.
# Nothing in the working tree reveals that it happened, which is why the
# check below is worth running rather than assuming.
#
# To verify after merging (uploader.py:134 is a line the reformat
# created by splitting a signature):
#
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#   git blame -L 134,134 obs_youtube_uploader/uploader.py
#
# It should name the author who wrote the line, NOT the reformat. If it
# names the reformat, the hash below is stale: delete the line rather
# than substituting whatever commit replaced it. A squash or rebase
# commit also covers the lint fixes, the CI changes, and the noqa fix,
# none of which should be hidden from blame.

